WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:05.910
All right, let's get talking about virtualization.

2
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.750
Virtualization is when things get really interesting

3
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:09.590
on the mainframe.

4
00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:13.510
So you've got this machine that is physically very big.

5
00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.270
The goal is always to make them smaller and smaller,

6
00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.230
but a mainframe actually does have a ton of processors,

7
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.870
memory, and IO in it.

8
00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.870
And it's got all the cooling and the power

9
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.950
and the network required to make it run.

10
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.030
So it's big, but that's a physical description.

11
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.790
Again, we're trying to draw this distinction

12
00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.790
between physical and logical, right?

13
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.150
So you've got a mainframe.

14
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.230
You've got this big, empty, logical area.

15
00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.150
We've defined all the physical stuff.

16
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.350
You've got all these processors, the storage, the IO adapter,

17
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.710
but not formed into anything yet.

18
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.830
So you start grabbing a couple of these processors.

19
00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.230
There's a little bit of memory, some connectivity,

20
00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.310
a couple of IO adapters.

21
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.190
You start squishing them all together and you say, OK,

22
00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.470
this is a new system right here.

23
00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.990
I've built a new system out of these physical components.

24
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.150
I've built a logical system here.

25
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.150
And for all intents and purposes, it is a computer.

26
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.110
You can start it up.

27
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.510
You can shut it down.

28
00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:08.830
It runs an operating system.

29
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.270
It has a display.

30
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.670
Well, it's a virtual display.

31
00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.270
You have to use some software to look at it,

32
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.990
but it's a computer.

33
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.150
And we call that a logical partition or an L-par.

34
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.270
So building an L-par, it's a lot like having this big,

35
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.830
plastic tub of building blocks, right?

36
00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.590
It's nothing built specifically,

37
00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.110
but you got all these resources here.

38
00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.030
So you can grab some pieces and you start

39
00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:30.870
sticking them together.

40
00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.910
And look, I made a boat.

41
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.510
And I'd take some more over here,

42
00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.190
start putting them together.

43
00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.270
And I've made a house, right?

44
00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.070
I can make a car.

45
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.990
And I can keep on going until I've run out of resources.

46
00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.710
So I do the same thing with physical resources

47
00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.150
on a mainframe to build these L-pars.

48
00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.350
Say I've got 16 L-pars, all built on my mainframe.

49
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.630
And some of them are really big with a bunch of processors

50
00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.110
and memory and IO, and some of them are really small,

51
00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.710
just enough just to run a basic program.

52
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.350
Some have a ton of processors,

53
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.030
but not a lot of memory.

54
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.950
Some have very few processors,

55
00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.870
but a ton of IO adapters.

56
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:07.950
And you can build them like that.

57
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.430
That's kind of the flexibility behind virtualization.

58
00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.990
Physically, the mainframe looks the same before

59
00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.230
and after you've done all that.

60
00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.630
It's just sits there and does its job, it's a mainframe.

61
00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:22.510
But logically, you've created 16 new systems.

62
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.750
And if you told somebody, hey,

63
00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.830
check out my system, log into this system I created.

64
00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.230
They wouldn't have any way of knowing

65
00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.750
that it's an L-par, sitting next to a whole bunch

66
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.750
of other systems within one big physical system,

67
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.110
because it's its own computer, its own system.

68
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.750
You've got one big physical mainframe

69
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.550
and 16 logical servers.

70
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.310
I do wanna talk a little bit more

71
00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.470
about resources on the mainframe,

72
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.910
particularly the processors.

73
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.510
There are a whole bunch of processor units, processors,

74
00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.190
and a processor can behave in a couple different ways,

75
00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.270
depending on how it's configured.

76
00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.230
It can be a processor, just like the one

77
00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.150
in a desktop computer or a laptop

78
00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.190
where it runs the operating system

79
00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.990
and helps with applications.

80
00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.470
And that's called a CP or a central processor.

81
00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:12.990
You can also have a system assist processor, SAP or SAP,

82
00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.390
and an SAP helps link between the CP and the I-O.

83
00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.950
So instead of the main processor having to worry

84
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.670
about shuffling data back and forth

85
00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.030
and saying, oh, take this byte off of here

86
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.310
and move it over here.

87
00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.710
The SAP processor handles all that stuff.

88
00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.190
It speeds things up tremendously.

89
00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.150
It lets the CP focus on the business application

90
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.030
that's running, not on the stuff in the background.

91
00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.710
And then there's the IFL, the integrated facility

92
00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.430
for Linux, which is a processor specifically

93
00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.670
for running Linux on the mainframe.

94
00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.470
Now you can run Linux on a CP processor,

95
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.710
a general purpose one, but the IFL

96
00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.390
has special licensing associated with it.

97
00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.230
So it's more cost-effective for running Linux.

98
00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.310
And then lastly, there's zip processors.

99
00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.790
That's Z-I-I-P. For certain workloads,

100
00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.390
you can use these zip processors

101
00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:04.230
to offload work to them, stuff like Java runs on zips.

102
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.190
And again, the purpose is to one,

103
00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.550
let the main processor offload their work,

104
00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.030
and two, enable more cost-effective option for workloads

105
00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.750
where it might be hard to predict or contain them.

106
00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.110
I mentioned earlier, the I-O adapters.

107
00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.710
Now the mainframe has a lot of I-O adapters.

108
00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.830
Each one of those wires you're seeing in this picture

109
00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:24.350
right here goes up to an I-O adapter.

110
00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.870
And there's even more on the back,

111
00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.750
and even more on the other frame.

112
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.230
On the other end, those are connected

113
00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.550
to disk storage devices, network adapters,

114
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.630
network attached storage, other mainframes, you name it.

115
00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.470
We identify these I-O adapters as channel path identifiers

116
00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.990
or chipids.

117
00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.270
The chipid is associated with a physical port location,

118
00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:49.110
also called a P-Chid for the physical channel ID,

119
00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.510
and a logical subsystem that it's associated with.

120
00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.190
The subsystem is the connection between the L-PAR

121
00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:57.710
and the I-O devices, or other L-PARs

122
00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.190
that it needs to communicate directly with.

123
00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.870
You can put everything into one big subsystem,

124
00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.830
or you can create a few logical subsystems within it.

125
00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.990
So the chipid, which is the association

126
00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:11.110
between the physical port location and the subsystem,

127
00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.110
you can think about it just like being a line between the two,

128
00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.350
can be dedicated fully to one L-PAR,

129
00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.470
meaning it has full control of it,

130
00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.710
or it can be shared between multiple L-PARs.

131
00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:26.070
It can also be unshared and reconfigurable,

132
00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.710
meaning that it can be switched from one L-PAR to another,

133
00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.230
but only one at a time, since it's a logical connection.

134
00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:35.550
And lastly, a chipid can also be configured as spanned,

135
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.630
which means that it's actually accessed

136
00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:41.430
from partitions from multiple logical channel subsystems.

137
00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.110
One last word about virtualization,

138
00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.150
that we're gonna take a very logical and physical break.

139
00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.110
In addition to hosting operating systems

140
00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.230
within L-PARs, there's an operating system

141
00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.510
that specializes in virtualization,

142
00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:56.710
called ZVM, or virtual machine.

143
00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.350
ZVM is a type one hypervisor,

144
00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.990
meaning that it doesn't need to sit on top

145
00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.870
of another operating system in order to do its thing.

146
00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.630
And within ZVM, you can create hundreds,

147
00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.590
thousands of virtual machines.

148
00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:12.430
They all share the resources that the host ZVM system has.

149
00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.750
And to the mainframe, it thinks it's just hosting

150
00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.070
one big operating system, but within it,

151
00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.670
you can run any number of operating systems,

152
00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:23.430
anything that runs on the mainframe runs under ZVM,

153
00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.430
including another ZVM.

154
00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.390
So that's virtually everything I have to tell you

155
00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.790
about virtualization on the mainframe.
